  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "For the curve $y=2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2},$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{1}{2}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$",
